.TH QUESYNTH 1 "October 2026" "Quesynth" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
quesynth \- native standalone Synth1-compatible synthesiser, with a daemon, a terminal UI, a browser front-end and a local MCP server
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B quesynth
.RB [ \-\-bank
.IR bank.json ]
.RI [ patch.sy1 ]
.br
.B quesynth \-\-daemon
.RB [ \-\-bank
.IR bank.json ]
.RI [ patch.sy1 ]
.br
.B quesynth \-\-browser
.RB [ \-\-bank
.IR bank.json ]
.RI [ patch.sy1 ]
.br
.B quesynth \-\-mcp
.br
.B quesynth \-\-stop
.br
.B quesynth \-\-selftest
.I patch.sy1 out.wav
.br
.B quesynth \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B quesynth
is the native standalone build of Quesynth.
A daemon owns the audio device, the engine, the MIDI inputs, the patch bank, the archive and a control socket.
Front-ends attach to it over that socket and can come and go without interrupting the sound: the terminal UI, a browser page and an MCP client.
.PP
Without a mode option,
.B quesynth
attaches the terminal UI to the daemon, and starts a detached daemon first when none is listening.
Quitting the UI leaves the daemon and its audio running.
.PP
The full reference is the Quesynth standalone manual,
.IR docs/quesynth-manual.md ,
in the source repository.
.SH MODES AND OPTIONS
.TP
.I no mode option
Attach the terminal UI, starting the daemon if needed.
It takes an optional
.B \-\-bank
.I bank.json
and an optional
.I patch.sy1
operand.
They apply only when this command starts the daemon.
.TP
.B \-\-daemon
Run the headless daemon in the foreground until Ctrl-C, SIGTERM or
.BR "quesynth \-\-stop" .
It plays
.I patch.sy1
if given, and the built-in defaults otherwise.
.B \-\-bank
names a bank of patches in JSON to browse instead of the factory bank.
A second daemon while one is listening prints a message and exits 0.
.TP
.B \-\-browser
Attach to or start the daemon, then serve the shared browser panel from a local Node.js adapter at 127.0.0.1:8177 and open it in the default browser.
It takes the same
.B \-\-bank
and
.I patch.sy1
operands as
.BR \-\-daemon .
Node.js 20 or later is required for this mode only.
.TP
.B \-\-mcp
Serve the Model Context Protocol over standard input and output, as a client of a running daemon's control socket.
It takes no operands or options.
See
.BR MCP .
.TP
.B \-\-stop
Ask a running daemon to shut down.
Prints
.B stop requested
and exits 0, or reports that no daemon is listening and exits 1.
.TP
.BI \-\-selftest " patch.sy1 out.wav"
Render the patch offline to a 48 kHz stereo WAV file without opening an audio or MIDI device.
Exits 0 only if the patch loaded and the file was written.
.TP
.BR \-\-help ", " \-h
Print the usage to standard output and exit 0.
.PP
Only the Linux build has a control socket.
On Windows the daemon plays and listens to MIDI, and the terminal UI,
.BR \-\-browser ,
.B \-\-stop
and
.B \-\-mcp
report that they are not supported there yet.
.SH MCP
.B quesynth \-\-mcp
speaks newline-delimited JSON-RPC 2.0 on standard input and output.
It holds no engine or parameter state and does not start audio or a daemon; the daemon stays the authority for parameter metadata and values.
It exits 0 when standard input closes.
.PP
The client must call
.B initialize
and send the
.B notifications/initialized
notification before it lists or calls anything else.
The protocol versions 2024-11-05, 2025-03-26, 2025-06-18 and 2025-11-25 are accepted.
Without a daemon,
.BR initialize ,
.BR ping ,
.B tools/list
and
.B resources/list
still work, and calls that need the daemon return
.BR daemon_unavailable .
.PP
There are exactly two tools:
.TP
.B inspect_synth
Read the daemon's parameter values and revision, its parameter registry and the sounding patch's identity.
It changes nothing.
.TP
.B apply_parameters
Set stored parameter values as one all-or-nothing batch, given as
.B parameters
(an array of
.B id
and
.B value
objects) and
.BR expected_revision .
The batch is applied only if the daemon's revision is still the one named, which is the
.B revision
reported by
.B inspect_synth
or by the previous successful apply.
Otherwise the call returns
.B revision_conflict
and changes nothing.
If a timeout or a disconnect leaves the outcome unknown, call
.B inspect_synth
before sending anything again; the server never repeats a request.
.PP
There are exactly two resources, both of type application/json:
.B quesynth://parameters
(the parameter registry) and
.B quesynth://patch
(the revision, the current values and the patch identity).
.PP
To register the server in an MCP client, use the command
.B quesynth
with the argument
.BR \-\-mcp ,
as the
.I .mcp.json
in the source repository does.
.B quesynth
must be on the client's
.BR PATH ,
for example by linking
.I build/quesynth
into
.IR ~/.local/bin .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
When set and not empty, the control socket is
.IR $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/quesynth/quesynth.sock ,
in a directory created with mode 0700.
The daemon, the terminal UI,
.B \-\-stop
and
.B \-\-mcp
use the same path, so they must run with the same value.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Directory under which the
.I quesynth
configuration directory lives.
Defaults to
.IR $HOME/.config .
.TP
.B HOME
Used for the configuration directory when
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is not set.
When neither is set, nothing is saved.
.TP
.B QUESYNTH_ROOT
The repository root, from which
.B \-\-browser
loads
.I ui/
and its adapter.
Defaults to the working directory.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set and not empty, the terminal UI uses no colour.
.PP
The
.B quesynth
binary does not read
.BR QUESYNTH_SOCKET ;
only the browser adapter does.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/quesynth/quesynth.sock
The daemon's control socket, mode 0600.
.TP
.I /tmp/quesynth-UID.sock
The control socket when
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
is unset or empty.
.I UID
is the numeric user id.
.TP
.I SOCKET.lock
A lock file beside the socket, held while a daemon runs.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/quesynth/
The configuration directory, or
.I ~/.config/quesynth/
when
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset.
It holds
.I archive.path
(the remembered ZIP archive),
.I bank.json
(the user bank),
.I config.conf
(settings written by the terminal UI) and
.I theme.conf
(the terminal UI's colours).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including end of input for
.BR \-\-mcp ,
a daemon that is already running when
.B \-\-daemon
is started again, and
.BR \-\-help .
.TP
.B 1
A run-time failure: no usable audio device, no daemon to reach or stop, a patch that cannot be loaded, a failed
.BR \-\-selftest ,
or a failed browser adapter, whose own exit status
.B \-\-browser
returns.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error: an unknown option, a missing or extra operand, or an operand after
.B \-\-stop
or
.BR \-\-mcp .
The error and the usage go to standard error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR docs/quesynth-manual.md ,
the Quesynth standalone manual, for the daemon, the terminal UI, banks and archives, MIDI, configuration and the MCP server in full.
